    title: "Water Dream Meaning",
    metaTitle: "Water Dream Meaning: Clear Water, Floods & Emotions",
    metaDescription: "What does water mean in a dream? Explore clear water, floods, muddy water, oceans, swimming, emotional symbolism, psychology, and personal context.",
    h1: "Water Dream Meaning",
    intro: "Water in a dream often brings emotional movement, cleansing, uncertainty, or overwhelm into view. Clear, calm water can feel very different from muddy water or a flood, so the condition of the water, your distance from it, and what you did in the scene offer a better starting point than one fixed definition. The dream does not predict an actual flood or future event.",
    ancientSymbolism: ["Across cultures, water has carried many meanings: life, cleansing, fertility, passage, danger, renewal, and the unknown. Wells, rivers, rain, oceans, and floods each appear in different traditions, but their meanings depend on the story and community in which they are used.", "A river that can be crossed is different from water that enters a home or rises without limit. These cultural images can help you think about change, boundaries, and renewal, but they should not be treated as omens or universal rules."],
    jungianPerspective: ["From a Jungian-inspired perspective, water can be explored as an image of emotional life and material outside ordinary awareness. Its depth, visibility, motion, and boundaries may show how close you feel to an emotion and whether it seems understandable, inviting, or difficult to contain.", "Clear water may suggest that something once confusing has become easier to see; a flood may give accumulated pressure a physical scale; deep or dark water can represent uncertainty without automatically meaning danger. Your response in the dream is as important as the water itself."],
    personalContext: ["Begin with what happened. Were you drinking, swimming, watching from shore, crossing, rescuing someone, protecting a house, or searching for higher ground? Each action describes a different relationship with what the water may represent.", "Real experiences matter too. Weather news, travel, swimming, thirst, a leaking pipe, fear of water, grief, or a place connected with family can shape the dream directly. Your own history should take priority over a generic dream dictionary meaning."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "What does clear water mean in a dream?", copy: "Clear water can bring clarity, relief, honesty, calm, or an emotion that is becoming easier to recognize into focus. Notice whether you were looking through it, drinking it, swimming in it, or afraid despite its clarity; those details change the reading." }, { title: "What does a flood mean in a dream?", copy: "A flood can give overload, rapid change, grief, conflict, or responsibilities that have crossed their usual boundaries a physical form. Where the water entered and what you tried to protect may identify the waking situation that feels hardest to contain." }, { title: "Muddy or dirty water", copy: "Cloudy water may reflect uncertainty, mixed feelings, limited information, or a situation in which you cannot yet see the consequences clearly. It does not mean something bad will happen; it may simply show that clarity is not available yet." }, { title: "Calm ocean, lake, or river", copy: "Calm water can suggest emotional space, restoration, acceptance, or a wish to pause after a demanding period. A large body of water can still feel awe-inspiring or lonely, so include the feeling and landscape in your reflection." }, { title: "Large waves or stormy water", copy: "Waves and storms can make emotion feel rhythmic, unpredictable, or larger than personal control. Ask whether you were bracing, surfing, escaping, watching safely, or being repeatedly pulled back." }, { title: "Swimming in water", copy: "Swimming may reflect how you are actively meeting an emotional situation. Ease can suggest confidence or trust, while exhaustion, currents, or difficulty reaching shore may point toward effort, depleted support, or an unclear destination." }, { title: "Water rising inside a house", copy: "When water enters a house, emotional pressure may feel connected with privacy, family, memory, or safety. The room where it appeared and whether you contained, escaped, or accepted the water can make the image more personal." }],
    reflectionQuestions: ["Was the water clear, muddy, calm, deep, stormy, or rising?", "Where was the water, and which part of life does that place represent to you?", "Were you observing, drinking, swimming, crossing, escaping, or protecting someone?", "What feeling remained after waking: peace, fear, grief, awe, relief, or urgency?", "What boundary, conversation, rest, or practical support could help the waking situation feel more manageable?"],
