    personalContext: ["Start with the place. Water entering a home may connect the dream with privacy, family, memory, or safety; a flooded street can bring shared pressure or disrupted plans into focus; an unknown landscape may emphasize uncertainty itself.", "Real experiences should take priority over symbolism. Weather reports, a past flood, a leaking home, grief, moving, financial pressure, caregiving, or a demanding week can all shape the dream directly. If you have experienced a real disaster, a recurring flood dream may be part of processing that event rather than a hidden message; support from a qualified professional can be more useful than a fixed interpretation when it remains distressing."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "What does a house flooding dream mean?", copy: "A flooded house can make emotional pressure feel close to home. Notice which room filled first, what you tried to save, and whether the house was your current home, a childhood home, or somewhere unfamiliar." }, { title: "Water rising from the floor or basement", copy: "Water rising from below can emphasize a concern, memory, or feeling that has been building outside everyday attention. The basement, foundation, or lowest room may point toward where the pressure first became noticeable." }, { title: "Rain or water leaking through the roof", copy: "A roof leak or heavy rain entering from above can focus the dream on protection that no longer feels sufficient. Ask whether one manageable problem slowly spread or whether many demands arrived at once." }, { title: "Rising floodwater in a street or city", copy: "Flooded roads and public spaces can make shared disruption, blocked plans, or uncertainty about the next route central. Notice whether other people helped, ignored the danger, or moved in a different direction." }, { title: "Escaping or surviving a flood", copy: "Reaching higher ground, finding a boat, or helping someone can bring adaptability and support into the scene. The route you found may suggest which resources or relationships currently help you regain perspective." }, { title: "Being swept away by floodwater", copy: "Being carried by water can express a loss of control, rapid change, or exhaustion from trying to resist what feels larger than you. Notice whether you fought the current, floated, reached for someone, or eventually found solid ground." }, { title: "Clear floodwater or muddy floodwater", copy: "Clear floodwater can combine overwhelm with unusual visibility, while muddy or dirty water may emphasize confusion, mixed feelings, or incomplete information. Neither condition predicts a positive or negative event." }, { title: "Protecting family, pets, or belongings", copy: "What you protect can reveal the value at the emotional center of the dream. It may point toward care, security, identity, memory, or the fear of being unable to meet every responsibility at once." }, { title: "A flood is coming but never reaches you", copy: "Waiting for approaching water can make anticipation more important than impact. Consider which change, deadline, conversation, or responsibility feels close enough to prepare for even if it has not happened." }, { title: "Why do I keep dreaming about floods?", copy: "Recurring flood dreams may return while the same pressure, memory, or boundary remains active. Compare the water level, setting, people, your response, and whether you reach safety instead of assuming repetition carries one fixed message." }],
    reflectionQuestions: ["Where did the flood begin, and which boundary did the water cross?", "Was the water clear, muddy, slow, violent, warm, or cold?", "What or whom did you try to protect, and why did it matter?", "Did you escape, freeze, help someone, surrender to the current, or find higher ground?", "What in waking life currently feels difficult to contain, and what practical support could make it more manageable?"],
