    jungianPerspective: ["From a Jungian-inspired perspective, the ocean can be explored as an image of emotional and psychological life that extends beyond what the conscious mind can fully map. Calm water may create space for contemplation, while darkness, depth, or waves can give uncertainty and powerful feeling a visible form.", "Your position changes the reading. Watching from shore, swimming, floating in open water, steering a boat, or being pulled by a current each expresses a different relationship to what feels vast. This is a reflective framework, not a diagnosis."],
    personalContext: ["Start with your real relationship to the ocean. A childhood beach, migration, a ferry trip, fear of deep water, recent weather news, a film, or a person connected with the coast may shape the dream more directly than a universal symbol.", "Then notice orientation and support. Could you see land? Were you alone, with family, on a boat, or beneath the surface? The answer may help you identify whether a waking situation feels spacious, unfamiliar, emotionally deep, or in need of steadier direction."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "Dreaming of a calm ocean", copy: "A calm sea can bring rest, emotional room, acceptance, or a wish for quiet into focus. If the stillness felt eerie rather than peaceful, it may instead emphasize uncertainty, loneliness, or waiting for change." }, { title: "Big ocean waves or rough seas", copy: "Large or crashing waves can make pressure, conflict, anticipation, or rapid change feel larger than personal control. Notice whether you watched from safety, braced for impact, rode the wave, or kept being pulled back." }, { title: "Swimming in the ocean", copy: "Swimming can reflect how actively you are meeting an emotional or uncertain situation. Easy movement may suggest trust or confidence, while exhaustion, strong currents, or difficulty reaching shore may point toward effort and depleted support." }, { title: "Being in deep or dark ocean water", copy: "Deep water can emphasize what is difficult to see, measure, or control. It does not automatically mean danger. Ask whether the depth inspired fear, awe, curiosity, or a desire to return to familiar ground." }, { title: "Standing on the beach or shore", copy: "The shoreline places you at a boundary between familiar ground and something larger. It may reflect contemplation, readiness, hesitation, or the need to observe before entering a change." }, { title: "Being far from land or lost at sea", copy: "An absent shore can give disorientation, isolation, or lack of a clear endpoint a physical setting. Look for what helped you navigate—a boat, star, person, current, or decision—and where similar guidance is needed in waking life." }, { title: "Traveling by boat on the ocean", copy: "A boat can represent a structure, relationship, plan, or skill that helps you move through uncertainty. Its condition and who shared it with you may reveal how supported and prepared you currently feel." }, { title: "Recurring ocean dreams", copy: "Repeated ocean dreams may return while the same emotional theme, transition, or memory remains active. Compare the tide, weather, distance from shore, companions, and your response rather than assuming the repetition carries one fixed message." }],
