    title: "House Dream Meaning",
    metaTitle: "House Dream Meaning: Rooms, Memory & the Inner Self",
    metaDescription: "What does a house dream mean? Explore childhood homes, unfamiliar houses, hidden rooms, old houses, safety, memory, psychology, and personal context.",
    h1: "House Dream Meaning",
    intro: "A house in a dream can feel familiar, impossible, welcoming, neglected, or full of rooms you have never seen. Because homes hold memory, privacy, identity, and safety, the condition of the house and where you went inside it often matter more than a single universal definition.",
    ancientSymbolism: ["Across stories and traditions, houses mark the boundary between inner and outer life. Doors, thresholds, hearths, roofs, and foundations can symbolize belonging, protection, inheritance, hospitality, and the order a person builds around daily life.", "These cultural associations do not make a house dream an omen. They offer a language for considering what currently feels secure, hidden, inherited, open to change, or in need of repair."],
    jungianPerspective: ["From a Jungian-inspired perspective, the house can be explored as an image of the psyche. Different floors, rooms, and forgotten spaces may represent parts of experience that are familiar, private, developing, or outside ordinary awareness.", "This is not a rigid map. A basement does not always mean one thing, and an attic does not always mean another. The room's history, atmosphere, contents, and your movement through it give the image its personal meaning."],
    personalContext: ["Begin with whose house it was and whether you had lived there. A childhood home can activate family memories or an earlier identity, while an unfamiliar house may reflect a new role, unexplored possibility, or uncertainty about where you belong.", "Notice what needed attention. Broken locks, flooding, extra rooms, renovations, intruders, or people gathering inside can bring boundaries, emotional pressure, potential, safety, and relationships into different kinds of focus."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "Dreaming about your childhood home", copy: "A childhood home may reconnect you with family patterns, earlier needs, belonging, or the person you were in that period. Notice whether the house looked as remembered or had changed." }, { title: "Finding hidden or extra rooms", copy: "Unexpected rooms can represent unused possibilities, memories, responsibilities, or parts of yourself that are becoming available to explore. What was inside matters more than the room alone." }, { title: "An unfamiliar house", copy: "A house you do not recognize may reflect entering a new life situation or discovering qualities that do not yet feel fully known. Ask whether you felt curious, lost, welcome, or unsafe." }, { title: "An old, damaged, or abandoned house", copy: "Damage may bring neglected needs, exhausted routines, old beliefs, or concerns about stability into focus. It does not predict literal damage to a home." }, { title: "Moving into a new house", copy: "Moving can emphasize transition, identity, opportunity, or the work of making an unfamiliar situation feel like your own. Notice what you brought with you and what was left behind." }],
    reflectionQuestions: ["Whose house was it, and what period of life did it evoke?", "Which room or feature held your attention, and what happened there?", "Did the house feel safe, exposed, crowded, empty, damaged, or full of possibility?", "What part of your inner or private life currently needs exploration, repair, or a clearer boundary?"],
