    jungianPerspective: ["From a Jungian-inspired perspective, fire may represent concentrated psychic energy—emotion, desire, anger, creativity, or transformation that has become difficult to ignore. Its boundaries often reveal whether that energy feels usable or overwhelming.", "Smoke without visible flame can bring uncertainty into focus, while escaping, watching, feeding, or extinguishing a fire may show different relationships to conflict, responsibility, and change."],
    personalContext: ["Start with what burned. Your current home may connect the intensity with present safety or private life; a childhood home may bring family history, early roles, or an older version of yourself closer. A workplace, forest, object, or small candle points toward a different field of experience. Then notice what you tried to save and whether anyone helped.", "Real experiences also matter. Recent fire news, an alarm, heat, smoke, grief, conflict, a move, or concern about safety may contribute directly to the dream and should not be forced into a symbolic explanation. If you have experienced a real fire and recurring dreams are distressing, practical support may be more helpful than symbolism alone."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "Dreaming of your house on fire", copy: "A burning home can connect intensity with safety, private life, identity, or family. It is not a warning about the building. Which room caught fire, whether the structure survived, and what you protected can refine the reflection." }, { title: "Your childhood home is on fire", copy: "A childhood home can connect the flames with early memories, family patterns, or a former identity. Fear may emphasize loss, while calm or relief may suggest readiness to change an old role. The dream does not erase or predict anything about your past." }, { title: "Family or someone you love is inside", copy: "Searching for or protecting another person can center love, responsibility, helplessness, or fear of failing someone. Ask whether you are carrying more responsibility than belongs to you and what real support would make care more sustainable." }, { title: "Escaping a house fire", copy: "Getting outside can shift the dream toward boundaries, survival, and creating distance from an intense situation. Notice whether you felt relief, guilt, grief, or a need to return for someone or something." }, { title: "Putting out a fire", copy: "Extinguishing flames may mirror an effort to calm conflict, manage anger, protect something important, or regain control. Notice whether the fire responded, who helped, and whether the effort exhausted you." }, { title: "A wildfire or forest fire", copy: "A spreading fire may express events or emotion moving faster than you can contain. It can also raise questions about what remains, clears, or begins again after major change." }, { title: "Smoke without visible flames", copy: "Smoke can suggest sensing tension or a problem before its source is clear. Ask what currently feels confusing, concealed, or difficult to name directly." }, { title: "Being burned by fire", copy: "Being burned can focus the dream on contact with something painful, intense, or too close. Consider where a boundary, risk, relationship, or ambition currently needs more care." }],
