    jungianPerspective: ["From a Jungian-inspired perspective, a tornado can be explored as a concentrated image of psychic pressure: feelings, conflict, or change that seem to gather faster than the conscious mind can organize them. The scene may reveal less about an outside event than about your relationship to uncertainty and control.", "Watching the funnel from a distance, being caught in it, or reaching shelter can each show a different position toward that pressure. A dream can make chaos visible without requiring you to treat it as a diagnosis or a prediction."],
    personalContext: ["Begin with the real context around the dream. Recent severe-weather news, a film, a move, family responsibilities, conflict, rapid change, or a memory of a real storm can all shape the image. If you have lived through a disaster and recurring nightmares are distressing, support from a qualified professional may be more useful than symbolic interpretation.", "Then notice the action. Were you warning someone, searching for safety, protecting a home, standing still, or discovering what remained afterward? Those details can point toward the kind of support, boundary, preparation, or conversation that would feel grounding now."],
    commonScenarios: [{ title: "Seeing a tornado in the distance", copy: "A distant tornado can make anticipation central. Consider what feels as though it is approaching—a difficult conversation, deadline, change, or emotion—and whether watching from a distance gave you time to prepare or left you frozen." }, { title: "A tornado is coming toward you", copy: "When the storm moves closer, the dream may amplify urgency or a fear that a situation is becoming unavoidable. Notice whether you ran, warned others, found a plan, or felt unable to move; each response changes the question the dream raises." }, { title: "Taking shelter from a tornado", copy: "Finding a basement, interior room, or other shelter can bring protection and coping resources into focus. Who was with you, who was missing, and whether the shelter felt secure may reveal how supported or responsible you feel during a demanding period." }, { title: "A tornado hits your house", copy: "A house can carry associations with safety, family life, privacy, and the inner world. Damage to it may reflect concern that a fast change is touching a place you depend on. It does not predict harm to your home or family." }, { title: "Surviving a tornado or seeing the aftermath", copy: "Surviving can shift the dream from pure alarm toward endurance, repair, and what remains important after a disruption. The objects, people, or feelings that survived may be more revealing than the destruction itself." }, { title: "Seeing multiple tornadoes", copy: "Several tornadoes may make competing pressures, uncertainty, or many moving parts feel central. Rather than searching for one fixed cause, ask which demands currently feel simultaneous and what could make the next step more manageable." }],
